{"title":"1999 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 6","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/6/3b6d5d23a7e91fb9f6c248fb4cd43111165540f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta ABC$\" width=\"57\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/d/19d89806ebccebcd45969a015168f89e751a95b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle B = 72^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"79\" height=\"15\" />. What is the sum, in degrees, of the other two angles?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/e/04e0b84bc660f7382218eaf8834561d8c2c72a15.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] usepackage(&quot;mathptmx&quot;);       size(4cm);                     pair A = (0.4,1.1); pair B = (0,0); pair C = (2,0);  draw(A--B--C--cycle, linewidth(1.5pt));  label(&quot;$72^\\circ$&quot;, B + 0.35*dir(30));  label(&quot;$A$&quot;, A + (0,0.14)); label(&quot;$B$&quot;, B + (0,-0.14)); label(&quot;$C$&quot;, C + (0,-0.14)); [/asy]\" width=\"192\" height=\"138\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/6/b26064cfce5e8101ca244d52e8326ca7b5934ede.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 144 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 72 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 108 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 110 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 288$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"414\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let x and y be the other two angles of the triangle.\n</p><p>The sum of the interior angles in a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Triangle\" title=\"Triangle\">triangle</a> is always <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/0/3f0cd95f95238159d02dbcea0aca632c2928991c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"37\" height=\"15\" />, so we have:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/e/abe4861dc5fc7f590c9245c00d656a0c17149ab9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x + y + 72^{\\circ} = 180^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"151\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Subtracting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/b/24b827998def193fbf2d8ec73227dc09a0a27e63.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$72^{\\circ}$\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" /> from both sides of the equation gives:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/0/cd0ad30f3500292a3e6f3be5b006526047d55a5f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x + y = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C)} 108}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"135\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)\" title=\"1999 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7)\">1999 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7)</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems\" title=\"1999 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"1999 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></t